{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"usage-of-tanstack-form-with-ui-libraries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usage of TanStack Form with UI Libraries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Form is a headless library, offering you complete flexibility to style it as you see fit. It's compatible with a wide range of UI libraries, including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Chakra UI"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Tailwind"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Material UI"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mantine"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"shadcn/ui"},{"type":"text","value":", or even plain CSS."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This guide focuses on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Chakra UI"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Material UI"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mantine"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"shadcn/ui"},{"type":"text","value":", but the concepts are applicable to any UI library of your choice."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"prerequisites","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prerequisites"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before integrating TanStack Form with a UI library, ensure the necessary dependencies are installed in your project:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Chakra UI"},{"type":"text","value":", follow the installation instructions on their "},{"type":"link","href":"https://chakra-ui.com/docs/get-started/installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"official site"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Material UI"},{"type":"text","value":", follow the installation instructions on their "},{"type":"link","href":"https://mui.com/material-ui/getting-started/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"official site"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mantine"},{"type":"text","value":", refer to their "},{"type":"link","href":"https://mantine.dev/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"documentation"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"shadcn/ui"},{"type":"text","value":", refer to their "},{"type":"link","href":"https://ui.shadcn.com/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"official site"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: While you can mix and match libraries, it's generally advisable to stick with one to maintain consistency and minimize bloat."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"example-with-mantine","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example with Mantine"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's an example demonstrating the integration of TanStack Form with Mantine components:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { TextInput, Checkbox } from '@mantine/core'\nimport { useForm } from '@tanstack/react-form'\n\nexport default function App() {\n  const { Field, handleSubmit, state } = useForm({\n    defaultValues: {\n      name: '',\n      isChecked: false,\n    },\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      // Handle form submission\n      console.log(value)\n    },\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <form\n        onSubmit={(e) => {\n          e.preventDefault()\n          handleSubmit()\n        }}\n      >\n        <Field\n          name=\"name\"\n          children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n            <TextInput\n              defaultValue={state.value}\n              onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n              onBlur={handleBlur}\n              placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n            />\n          )}\n        />\n        <Field\n          name=\"isChecked\"\n          children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n            <Checkbox\n              onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.checked)}\n              onBlur={handleBlur}\n              checked={state.value}\n            />\n          )}\n        />\n      </form>\n      <div>\n        <pre>{JSON.stringify(state.values, null, 2)}</pre>\n      </div>\n    </>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initially, we utilize the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useForm"},{"type":"text","value":" hook from TanStack and destructure the necessary properties. This step is optional; alternatively, you could use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"const form = useForm()"},{"type":"text","value":" if preferred. TypeScript's type inference ensures a smooth experience regardless of the approach."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Field"},{"type":"text","value":" component, derived from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useForm"},{"type":"text","value":", accepts several properties, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validators"},{"type":"text","value":". For this demonstration, we focus on two primary properties: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"children"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" property identifies each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Field"},{"type":"text","value":", for instance, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" in our example."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"children"},{"type":"text","value":" property leverages the concept of render props, allowing us to integrate components without unnecessary abstractions."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack's design relies heavily on render props, providing access to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"children"},{"type":"text","value":" within the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Field"},{"type":"text","value":" component. This approach is entirely type-safe. When integrating with Mantine components, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TextInput"},{"type":"text","value":", we selectively destructure properties like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.value"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"handleChange"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"handleBlur"},{"type":"text","value":". This selective approach is due to the slight differences in types between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TextInput"},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" we get in the children."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By following these steps, you can seamlessly integrate Mantine components with TanStack Form."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This methodology is equally applicable to other components, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Checkbox"},{"type":"text","value":", ensuring consistent integration across different UI elements."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"usage-with-material-ui","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usage with Material UI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The process for integrating Material UI components is similar. Here's an example using TextField and Checkbox from Material UI:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<Field\n  name=\"name\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => {\n    return (\n      <TextField\n        id=\"filled-basic\"\n        label=\"Filled\"\n        variant=\"filled\"\n        defaultValue={state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n        onBlur={handleBlur}\n        placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n      />\n    );\n  }}\n/>\n\n<Field\n  name=\"isMuiCheckBox\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => {\n    return (\n      <MuiCheckbox\n        onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.checked)}\n        onBlur={handleBlur}\n        checked={state.value}\n      />\n    );\n  }}\n/>\n","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The integration approach is the same as with Mantine."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The primary difference lies in the specific Material UI component properties and styling options."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"usage-with-shadcn-ui","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usage with shadcn/ui"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The process for integrating shadcn/ui components is similar. Here's an example using Input and Checkbox from shadcn/ui:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<Field\n  name=\"name\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Input\n      value={state.value}\n      onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n      placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n<Field\n  name=\"isChecked\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Checkbox\n      onCheckedChange={(checked) => handleChange(checked === true)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n      checked={state.value}\n    />\n  )}\n/>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The integration approach is the same as with Mantine, Material UI."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The primary difference lies in the specific shadcn/ui component properties and styling options."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note the onCheckedChange property of Checkbox instead of onChange."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ShadCN library includes a dedicated guide covering common scenarios for integrating TanStack Form with its components: "},{"type":"link","href":"https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/forms/tanstack-form","children":[{"type":"text","value":"https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/forms/tanstack-form"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"usage-with-chakra-ui","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usage with Chakra UI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The process for integrating Chakra UI components is similar. Here's an example using Input and Checkbox from Chakra UI:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<Field\n  name=\"name\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Input\n      value={state.value}\n      onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n      placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n<Field\n  name=\"isChecked\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Checkbox.Root\n      checked={state.value}\n      onCheckedChange={(details) => handleChange(!!details.checked)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n    >\n      <Checkbox.HiddenInput />\n      <Checkbox.Control />\n      <Checkbox.Label>Accept terms</Checkbox.Label>\n    </Checkbox.Root>\n  )}\n/>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The integration approach is the same as with Mantine, Material UI, and shadcn/ui."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chakra UI exposes its Checkbox as a composable component with separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Checkbox.Root"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Checkbox.Control"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Checkbox.Label"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Checkbox.HiddenInput"},{"type":"text","value":" parts that you wire together."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The double negation "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"!!"},{"type":"text","value":" is used on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onCheckedChange"},{"type":"text","value":" to coerce Chakra's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"indeterminate\""},{"type":"text","value":" state to a boolean, ensuring it matches the form state. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://chakra-ui.com/docs/components/checkbox#indeterminate","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chakra UI Checkbox documentation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for more details."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, Chakra UI offers a pre-composed Checkbox component that works the same way as their standard examples, without requiring manual composition. You can learn more about this closed component approach in the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://chakra-ui.com/docs/components/checkbox#closed-component","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chakra UI Checkbox documentation"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The TanStack Form integration works identically with either approach—simply attach the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"checked"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onCheckedChange"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onBlur"},{"type":"text","value":" handlers to your chosen component."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example using the closed Checkbox component:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<Field\n  name=\"isChecked\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Checkbox\n      checked={state.value}\n      onCheckedChange={(details) => handleChange(!!details.checked)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n    >\n      Accept terms\n    </Checkbox>\n  )}\n/>","lang":"tsx"}],"headings":[{"id":"usage-of-tanstack-form-with-ui-libraries","text":"Usage of TanStack Form with UI Libraries","level":2},{"id":"prerequisites","text":"Prerequisites","level":3},{"id":"example-with-mantine","text":"Example with Mantine","level":3},{"id":"usage-with-material-ui","text":"Usage with Material UI","level":3},{"id":"usage-with-shadcn-ui","text":"Usage with shadcn/ui","level":3},{"id":"usage-with-chakra-ui","text":"Usage with Chakra UI","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Usage of TanStack Form with UI Libraries\n\nTanStack Form is a headless library, offering you complete flexibility to style it as you see fit. It's compatible with a wide range of UI libraries, including `Chakra UI`, `Tailwind`, `Material UI`, `Mantine`, `shadcn/ui`, or even plain CSS.\n\nThis guide focuses on `Chakra UI`, `Material UI`, `Mantine`, and `shadcn/ui`, but the concepts are applicable to any UI library of your choice.\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nBefore integrating TanStack Form with a UI library, ensure the necessary dependencies are installed in your project:\n\n- For `Chakra UI`, follow the installation instructions on their [official site](https://chakra-ui.com/docs/get-started/installation)\n- For `Material UI`, follow the installation instructions on their [official site](https://mui.com/material-ui/getting-started/).\n- For `Mantine`, refer to their [documentation](https://mantine.dev/).\n- For `shadcn/ui`, refer to their [official site](https://ui.shadcn.com/).\n\nNote: While you can mix and match libraries, it's generally advisable to stick with one to maintain consistency and minimize bloat.\n\n### Example with Mantine\n\nHere's an example demonstrating the integration of TanStack Form with Mantine components:\n\n```tsx\nimport { TextInput, Checkbox } from '@mantine/core'\nimport { useForm } from '@tanstack/react-form'\n\nexport default function App() {\n  const { Field, handleSubmit, state } = useForm({\n    defaultValues: {\n      name: '',\n      isChecked: false,\n    },\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      // Handle form submission\n      console.log(value)\n    },\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <form\n        onSubmit={(e) => {\n          e.preventDefault()\n          handleSubmit()\n        }}\n      >\n        <Field\n          name=\"name\"\n          children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n            <TextInput\n              defaultValue={state.value}\n              onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n              onBlur={handleBlur}\n              placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n            />\n          )}\n        />\n        <Field\n          name=\"isChecked\"\n          children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n            <Checkbox\n              onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.checked)}\n              onBlur={handleBlur}\n              checked={state.value}\n            />\n          )}\n        />\n      </form>\n      <div>\n        <pre>{JSON.stringify(state.values, null, 2)}</pre>\n      </div>\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n- Initially, we utilize the `useForm` hook from TanStack and destructure the necessary properties. This step is optional; alternatively, you could use `const form = useForm()` if preferred. TypeScript's type inference ensures a smooth experience regardless of the approach.\n- The `Field` component, derived from `useForm`, accepts several properties, such as `validators`. For this demonstration, we focus on two primary properties: `name` and `children`.\n  - The `name` property identifies each `Field`, for instance, `name` in our example.\n  - The `children` property leverages the concept of render props, allowing us to integrate components without unnecessary abstractions.\n- TanStack's design relies heavily on render props, providing access to `children` within the `Field` component. This approach is entirely type-safe. When integrating with Mantine components, such as `TextInput`, we selectively destructure properties like `state.value`, `handleChange`, and `handleBlur`. This selective approach is due to the slight differences in types between `TextInput` and the `field` we get in the children.\n- By following these steps, you can seamlessly integrate Mantine components with TanStack Form.\n- This methodology is equally applicable to other components, such as `Checkbox`, ensuring consistent integration across different UI elements.\n\n### Usage with Material UI\n\nThe process for integrating Material UI components is similar. Here's an example using TextField and Checkbox from Material UI:\n\n```tsx\n<Field\n  name=\"name\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => {\n    return (\n      <TextField\n        id=\"filled-basic\"\n        label=\"Filled\"\n        variant=\"filled\"\n        defaultValue={state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n        onBlur={handleBlur}\n        placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n      />\n    );\n  }}\n/>\n\n<Field\n  name=\"isMuiCheckBox\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => {\n    return (\n      <MuiCheckbox\n        onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.checked)}\n        onBlur={handleBlur}\n        checked={state.value}\n      />\n    );\n  }}\n/>\n\n```\n\n- The integration approach is the same as with Mantine.\n- The primary difference lies in the specific Material UI component properties and styling options.\n\n### Usage with shadcn/ui\n\nThe process for integrating shadcn/ui components is similar. Here's an example using Input and Checkbox from shadcn/ui:\n\n```tsx\n<Field\n  name=\"name\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Input\n      value={state.value}\n      onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n      placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n<Field\n  name=\"isChecked\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Checkbox\n      onCheckedChange={(checked) => handleChange(checked === true)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n      checked={state.value}\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n```\n\n- The integration approach is the same as with Mantine, Material UI.\n- The primary difference lies in the specific shadcn/ui component properties and styling options.\n- Note the onCheckedChange property of Checkbox instead of onChange.\n\nThe ShadCN library includes a dedicated guide covering common scenarios for integrating TanStack Form with its components: [https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/forms/tanstack-form](https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/forms/tanstack-form)\n\n### Usage with Chakra UI\n\nThe process for integrating Chakra UI components is similar. Here's an example using Input and Checkbox from Chakra UI:\n\n```tsx\n<Field\n  name=\"name\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Input\n      value={state.value}\n      onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n      placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n<Field\n  name=\"isChecked\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Checkbox.Root\n      checked={state.value}\n      onCheckedChange={(details) => handleChange(!!details.checked)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n    >\n      <Checkbox.HiddenInput />\n      <Checkbox.Control />\n      <Checkbox.Label>Accept terms</Checkbox.Label>\n    </Checkbox.Root>\n  )}\n/>\n```\n\n- The integration approach is the same as with Mantine, Material UI, and shadcn/ui.\n- Chakra UI exposes its Checkbox as a composable component with separate `Checkbox.Root`, `Checkbox.Control`, `Checkbox.Label`, and `Checkbox.HiddenInput` parts that you wire together.\n- The double negation `!!` is used on `onCheckedChange` to coerce Chakra's `\"indeterminate\"` state to a boolean, ensuring it matches the form state. See the [Chakra UI Checkbox documentation](https://chakra-ui.com/docs/components/checkbox#indeterminate) for more details.\n- Alternatively, Chakra UI offers a pre-composed Checkbox component that works the same way as their standard examples, without requiring manual composition. You can learn more about this closed component approach in the [Chakra UI Checkbox documentation](https://chakra-ui.com/docs/components/checkbox#closed-component).\n- The TanStack Form integration works identically with either approach—simply attach the `checked`, `onCheckedChange`, and `onBlur` handlers to your chosen component.\n\nExample using the closed Checkbox component:\n\n```tsx\n<Field\n  name=\"isChecked\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Checkbox\n      checked={state.value}\n      onCheckedChange={(details) => handleChange(!!details.checked)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n    >\n      Accept terms\n    </Checkbox>\n  )}\n/>\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: ui-libraries\ntitle: UI Libraries\n---\n\n## Usage of TanStack Form with UI Libraries\n\nTanStack Form is a headless library, offering you complete flexibility to style it as you see fit. It's compatible with a wide range of UI libraries, including `Chakra UI`, `Tailwind`, `Material UI`, `Mantine`, `shadcn/ui`, or even plain CSS.\n\nThis guide focuses on `Chakra UI`, `Material UI`, `Mantine`, and `shadcn/ui`, but the concepts are applicable to any UI library of your choice.\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nBefore integrating TanStack Form with a UI library, ensure the necessary dependencies are installed in your project:\n\n- For `Chakra UI`, follow the installation instructions on their [official site](https://chakra-ui.com/docs/get-started/installation)\n- For `Material UI`, follow the installation instructions on their [official site](https://mui.com/material-ui/getting-started/).\n- For `Mantine`, refer to their [documentation](https://mantine.dev/).\n- For `shadcn/ui`, refer to their [official site](https://ui.shadcn.com/).\n\nNote: While you can mix and match libraries, it's generally advisable to stick with one to maintain consistency and minimize bloat.\n\n### Example with Mantine\n\nHere's an example demonstrating the integration of TanStack Form with Mantine components:\n\n```tsx\nimport { TextInput, Checkbox } from '@mantine/core'\nimport { useForm } from '@tanstack/react-form'\n\nexport default function App() {\n  const { Field, handleSubmit, state } = useForm({\n    defaultValues: {\n      name: '',\n      isChecked: false,\n    },\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      // Handle form submission\n      console.log(value)\n    },\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <form\n        onSubmit={(e) => {\n          e.preventDefault()\n          handleSubmit()\n        }}\n      >\n        <Field\n          name=\"name\"\n          children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n            <TextInput\n              defaultValue={state.value}\n              onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n              onBlur={handleBlur}\n              placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n            />\n          )}\n        />\n        <Field\n          name=\"isChecked\"\n          children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n            <Checkbox\n              onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.checked)}\n              onBlur={handleBlur}\n              checked={state.value}\n            />\n          )}\n        />\n      </form>\n      <div>\n        <pre>{JSON.stringify(state.values, null, 2)}</pre>\n      </div>\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n- Initially, we utilize the `useForm` hook from TanStack and destructure the necessary properties. This step is optional; alternatively, you could use `const form = useForm()` if preferred. TypeScript's type inference ensures a smooth experience regardless of the approach.\n- The `Field` component, derived from `useForm`, accepts several properties, such as `validators`. For this demonstration, we focus on two primary properties: `name` and `children`.\n  - The `name` property identifies each `Field`, for instance, `name` in our example.\n  - The `children` property leverages the concept of render props, allowing us to integrate components without unnecessary abstractions.\n- TanStack's design relies heavily on render props, providing access to `children` within the `Field` component. This approach is entirely type-safe. When integrating with Mantine components, such as `TextInput`, we selectively destructure properties like `state.value`, `handleChange`, and `handleBlur`. This selective approach is due to the slight differences in types between `TextInput` and the `field` we get in the children.\n- By following these steps, you can seamlessly integrate Mantine components with TanStack Form.\n- This methodology is equally applicable to other components, such as `Checkbox`, ensuring consistent integration across different UI elements.\n\n### Usage with Material UI\n\nThe process for integrating Material UI components is similar. Here's an example using TextField and Checkbox from Material UI:\n\n```tsx\n<Field\n  name=\"name\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => {\n    return (\n      <TextField\n        id=\"filled-basic\"\n        label=\"Filled\"\n        variant=\"filled\"\n        defaultValue={state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n        onBlur={handleBlur}\n        placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n      />\n    );\n  }}\n/>\n\n<Field\n  name=\"isMuiCheckBox\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => {\n    return (\n      <MuiCheckbox\n        onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.checked)}\n        onBlur={handleBlur}\n        checked={state.value}\n      />\n    );\n  }}\n/>\n\n```\n\n- The integration approach is the same as with Mantine.\n- The primary difference lies in the specific Material UI component properties and styling options.\n\n### Usage with shadcn/ui\n\nThe process for integrating shadcn/ui components is similar. Here's an example using Input and Checkbox from shadcn/ui:\n\n```tsx\n<Field\n  name=\"name\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Input\n      value={state.value}\n      onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n      placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n<Field\n  name=\"isChecked\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Checkbox\n      onCheckedChange={(checked) => handleChange(checked === true)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n      checked={state.value}\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n```\n\n- The integration approach is the same as with Mantine, Material UI.\n- The primary difference lies in the specific shadcn/ui component properties and styling options.\n- Note the onCheckedChange property of Checkbox instead of onChange.\n\nThe ShadCN library includes a dedicated guide covering common scenarios for integrating TanStack Form with its components: [https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/forms/tanstack-form](https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/forms/tanstack-form)\n\n### Usage with Chakra UI\n\nThe process for integrating Chakra UI components is similar. Here's an example using Input and Checkbox from Chakra UI:\n\n```tsx\n<Field\n  name=\"name\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Input\n      value={state.value}\n      onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n      placeholder=\"Enter your name\"\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n<Field\n  name=\"isChecked\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Checkbox.Root\n      checked={state.value}\n      onCheckedChange={(details) => handleChange(!!details.checked)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n    >\n      <Checkbox.HiddenInput />\n      <Checkbox.Control />\n      <Checkbox.Label>Accept terms</Checkbox.Label>\n    </Checkbox.Root>\n  )}\n/>\n```\n\n- The integration approach is the same as with Mantine, Material UI, and shadcn/ui.\n- Chakra UI exposes its Checkbox as a composable component with separate `Checkbox.Root`, `Checkbox.Control`, `Checkbox.Label`, and `Checkbox.HiddenInput` parts that you wire together.\n- The double negation `!!` is used on `onCheckedChange` to coerce Chakra's `\"indeterminate\"` state to a boolean, ensuring it matches the form state. See the [Chakra UI Checkbox documentation](https://chakra-ui.com/docs/components/checkbox#indeterminate) for more details.\n- Alternatively, Chakra UI offers a pre-composed Checkbox component that works the same way as their standard examples, without requiring manual composition. You can learn more about this closed component approach in the [Chakra UI Checkbox documentation](https://chakra-ui.com/docs/components/checkbox#closed-component).\n- The TanStack Form integration works identically with either approach—simply attach the `checked`, `onCheckedChange`, and `onBlur` handlers to your chosen component.\n\nExample using the closed Checkbox component:\n\n```tsx\n<Field\n  name=\"isChecked\"\n  children={({ state, handleChange, handleBlur }) => (\n    <Checkbox\n      checked={state.value}\n      onCheckedChange={(details) => handleChange(!!details.checked)}\n      onBlur={handleBlur}\n    >\n      Accept terms\n    </Checkbox>\n  )}\n/>\n```\n"}
